it might be the chaos of the petrol  pumps making headlines but the supply  chain is being stretched at every step  a lack of skilled workers is already  delaying orders to supermarkets and  other smaller retailers  shane brennan represents businesses with  30 000 vehicles in the uk  they move chilled and frozen goods from  supplier to supermarket and have been  feeling the strain for months we're  firefighting at every stage of the  supply chain and so what that means is  that whereas we would normally pride  ourselves on being 98 order on time  we're operating around 75 80 percent  capacity  the government has tried to reassure the  public to carry on as normal but still  people are panicking for almost two  years they've experienced the trauma of  the pandemic and seen food shortages  which makes people more inclined to look  after their own  so according to experts panic buying is  a normal reaction it's been two years  nearly of constant changing of ideas and  not blaming anyone for that it's the  reality but psychologically that meant  that a lot of us feel completely out of  control of our own environment so  anything we can do whether that's  stocking up on toilet roll and lentils  and tim beans to getting petrol we're  going to do because there is a  selfishness about human beings that is  normal it's natural i want to protect my  family more than necessarily the next  family it's not that i'm not caring it's  that i care about my own first  but logistics experts say consumers will  have to change the way they behave to  stop the current crisis getting worse  what happened last year when everyone  panic bought in the grocery supply chain  in february and march is that we saw  huge amounts of food waste because  people bought way more than they needed  and they had to throw it all away and  that's just craziness we all need to  basically bet we've stuck together  through the pandemic up to now let's do  it still continue right the way through  to christmas and beyond  while plans are in place to resolve the  problems with the supply chain it will  take a long time and patience to get  fully back on track charlotte lomas sky  news
 

